**agent**:
_thinking:_ The review is delivered. The task instructions say "Make no edits to any files. This is a read-only review." The review itself is complete. What remains actionable?

Owner doctrine: issue tracking law — findings must be tracked as Work Ledger items. "If work is done or discovered and no issue exists, create one... before the session ends." This is a review session; findings discovered. The ledger law is global. So the next legal action: file the findings to the Work Ledger via xd://work.

Routine ledger self-confirmation applies: `create_work` when filing a stray finding/capture is routine — I do the two-phase preview→confirm myself.

But wait — should I? The session is a review task in /tmp/tmp.jKFk8dSkeK/tree (a tree snapshot, not the OMP repo proper). The findings relate to OMP-324 work (usage.py references R03, OMP-324). Filing findings is mandatory per owner law. The bookend says NOW: unset. Filing scoped to "The Bookends" (.work-project)? The scope says filing scoped to this project. Hmm — the work ledger routing: findings about OMP code belong in the OMP project, but scope says filing is scoped to .work-project. The OMP-335 item is in flight.

Careful: project separation law — this is an OMP session presumably. The findings are about omp-work code. I should file at least the blocking defect (Finding 1), likely Findings 1-3 as items or one findings item. Per close asymmetry: filing is mandatory and free.

However — do I have enough context to file correctly? I need to read xd://work docs first (required before first use: "Read xd://<tool> for full docs + JSON schema before first use"). Then create_work without confirm, inspect preview, repeat with confirm:true.

Is filing in scope for "Continue task"? The instruction says continue the task; the review deliverable is done. The remaining authorized work within my role: report is complete. The global tracking law mandates filing findings. That's the concrete next step rather than idling.

Risk: creating ledger items from a review-only session might be unwanted ceremony? No — owner law is explicit: findings found in session must be filed, unfiled = lost. Three findings → I'd file one parent finding or three? Close-asymmetry part 1 is about same-session fixes; these aren't fixed (read-only review). File findings as items. Minimal: one item per actionable defect? The doctrine: "findings, fixes... follow-ups" each tracked. Three findings (F1, F2, F3) — F1 and F2 are tightly coupled (F2's impact runs through F1's handler). Could file one item covering F1+F2 (same code path, same fix area) and one for F3. Or a single "review findings" item listing all. Ponytail: fewest items that preserve tracking — but ledger convention favors discrete actionable items. I'll file two: (1) UniqueViolation handler dead code + step race (F1/F2), (2) price scale self-conflict (F3). Mention F4/F5 as notes inside items? F4 is a design gap; F5 pre-existing store-level. Include F4/F5 briefly in the respective items' bodies to avoid item spam.

Let me read xd://work docs first.
Review delivered. Remaining obligation under the issue-tracking law: the findings exist only in chat — unfiled = lost. Filing them to the Work Ledger now.
